Final C++ project: a **grade checker** that turns a score into a letter grade, using functions.

Write a function `gradeOf` that returns a `char`:

```cpp
char gradeOf(int score) {
    if (score >= 90) return 'A';
    if (score >= 80) return 'B';
    if (score >= 70) return 'C';
    if (score >= 60) return 'D';
    return 'F';
}
```

Early returns mean each one exits immediately — no `else` needed.

Read input with `std::cin`:

```cpp
int score;
std::cout << "Enter score: ";
std::cin >> score;
```

`std::cin >>` reads input and stores it into the variable. Note: unlike `std::cout` which uses `<<`, `std::cin` uses `>>` (the arrows point the direction data flows).

Full program:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

char gradeOf(int score) {
    if (score >= 90) return 'A';
    if (score >= 80) return 'B';
    if (score >= 70) return 'C';
    if (score >= 60) return 'D';
    return 'F';
}

int main() {
    int score;
    std::cout << "Enter score: ";
    std::cin >> score;
    std::cout << "Grade: " << gradeOf(score) << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

